{id:'ch11-gen-5',title:'Post-buckling concept',level:'application',kind:'reasoning',q:'Does initial shear buckling of a suitably stiffened slender web necessarily mean immediate loss of all girder resistance?',choices:['No, post-buckling tension-field action can provide additional resistance','Yes, the web instantly carries zero force','Yes, both flanges must fracture immediately','No, because the web never buckles'],answer:0,explanation:'The chapter explicitly uses post-buckling tension-field action where the surrounding system can anchor it.',source:S},
{id:'ch11-gen-6',title:'Design mindset',level:'retrieval',kind:'concept',q:'What must be balanced in economical plate-girder design?',choices:['Strength, stability and fabrication efficiency','Only maximum plate thickness','Only steel density','Only flange colour'],answer:0,explanation:'Plate girder design is strongly influenced by thin-plate stability as well as ordinary strength and practical fabrication.',source:S}
